<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Swabian Jura</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Swabian Alb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Swabian Jura, or Swabian Alb, is a limestone upland in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, running about 200 km from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordlinger-ries">Ries crater</a>. Its steep northern edge was crowned by medieval castles such as Hohenzollern, Teck and Helfenstein, seats of noble families, while imperial towns such as Reutlingen grew at its foot.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/swabian-jura.jpg" alt="Swabian Jura" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thoroe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schw%C3%A4bische_Alb_-_Deutsche_Mittelgebirge,_Serie_A-de.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Swabian Jura, in German the Schwäbische Alb, lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> and runs from south-west to north-east, 40 to 70 km wide. It is bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the south-east and the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> in the north-west, and rises in the south-west towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a>. Its highest point is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lemberg</a>, at 1,015 metres. The profile is that of a high plateau that falls slowly to the south-east, with a steep forested escarpment, the Albtrauf, rising about 400 metres on the north-western side.</p><p>The rock is mostly limestone that formed the seabed in the Jurassic period. Rain seeps through cracks into a karst landscape of caves and underground rivers, so the plateau has hardly any surface water, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> nearly disappears near Immendingen. The soil is thin and not very fertile. Fossils are found throughout, notably around Holzmaden, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordlinger-ries">Nördlinger Ries</a> is a meteorite crater 15 million years old.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Caves of the region, among them Vogelherd, Hohlenstein-Stadel, Geißenklösterle and Hohle Fels, have yielded some of the oldest known human artifacts: figures of a mammoth, a horse's head, a water bird and a lion man, all more than 30,000 years old. Flutes made from the bones of swans and griffon vultures about 35,000 years old are the oldest known musical instruments, and a flute carved from mammoth tusk dates from around 37,000 years ago. More than 3,000 engraved signs on about 260 Aurignacian artifacts, dated to roughly 40,000 to 34,000 years ago, show structured patterns that may point to a conventional system of graphic signs.</p><p>The region, south of the limes, was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> from around the first to the third century. A Roman road ran along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> and over the plateau, and the limes cuts across it at Aalen, named after a Roman cavalry post, the ala. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> castles stood along the escarpment, among them Hohenzollern, Teck and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, and towns such as Reutlingen lay in the foreland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Life on the plateau was long hard, because water was short and the soil poor. Many villages fetched water on long journeys until a modern water supply was built in the late 1880s. Sheep farming gave rise to a textile industry, from which machine building, the motor industry and electronics later grew, and the region has changed from one of the poorest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to one of the richest within about 150 years.</p><p>People speak a distinct Swabian German dialect, and the Swabian-Alemannic carnival, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-alemannic-fastnacht">Fasnet</a>, is an important tradition, notably at Rottweil. The whole area is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> global geopark covering 6,688 square kilometres, with a biosphere reserve at its centre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Swabian Jura” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their castle stood on the steep Zollern hill, an outlier of the <b>Swabian Jura</b> above the town of Hechingen, and from it the family built up rights over lands and churches between the upper Neckar and the Danube.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The lords of Helfenstein appear around 1100 with a castle on the <b>Swabian Jura</b> above Geislingen an der Steige.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-helfenstein">County of Helfenstein</a></li><li><blockquote>Adalbert, a younger son of Duke Conrad of Zähringen, inherited lands around the castle of Teck on the edge of the <b>Swabian Jura</b> and called himself Duke of Teck from 1187.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-teck">Duchy of Teck</a></li><li><blockquote>Reutlingen grew at the foot of the Achalm hill on the edge of the <b>Swabian Jura</b>, and was fortified and granted town rights by the Hohenstaufen around 1240, becoming an imperial city.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-reutlingen">Free Imperial City of Reutlingen</a></li></ul></section>
